exec(open('/home/claude/work/gita/gc.py').read())
V=[
X(1,'THE TRUE SANNYĀSĪ AND YOGI',
 S('A householder doing his work, glowing like a monk','P','light','SANNYĀSĪ · YOGĪ'),
 'The Lord said: One who does the work that is to be done without depending on its fruit is a renunciant and a yogi; not one who merely gives up the sacred fire and all activity.',
 [('anāśritaḥ karma-phalam','<b>not depending on the fruit of action</b>'),('kāryam karma karoti yaḥ','<b>who does the work to be done</b>'),('saḥ sannyāsī ca yogī ca','<b>he is a renunciant and a yogi</b>'),('na niragniḥ na ca akriyaḥ','<b>not one without fire or activity</b>')],
 'Chapter 6, on meditation, opens by repeating chapter 5’s lesson: renunciation is inner.',
 'A renunciant traditionally gave up the household fire (<i>agni</i>). Kṛṣṇa says this outer act alone does not make one a sannyāsī. Commentators note the Gītā keeps returning to this point because it is easily misunderstood.',
 'You don’t need to give up your life to be spiritual. Give up clinging to results.',
 'Renunciation is <b>inner, not outer</b>.'),
X(2,'RENUNCIATION IS YOGA',
 S('A person letting go of a tangle of plans','Y','mind','LET GO OF SCHEMES'),
 '“Know that what they call renunciation is yoga, Pāṇḍava; for no one becomes a yogi without renouncing selfish schemes (saṅkalpa).”',
 [('yam sannyāsam iti prāhuḥ','<b>what they call renunciation</b>'),('yogam tam viddhi pāṇḍava','<b>know that as yoga</b>'),('na hi asannyasta-saṅkalpaḥ','<b>for without renouncing schemes</b>'),('yogī bhavati kaścana','<b>no one becomes a yogi</b>')],
 'Renunciation and yoga are the same at heart: giving up selfish planning.',
 '<i>Saṅkalpa</i> means the mind’s plans and wishes for the future. Commentators say the root of restlessness is constant scheming; letting it go is the start of meditation.',
 'Notice how much of the mind is busy planning gains. Letting some of that go brings rest.',
 'Drop the schemes: <b>that is yoga</b>.'),
X(3,'CLIMBING AND ARRIVING',
 S('A person climbing a hill by work, then sitting at the top in calm','Y','mountain','ĀRURUKṢU · ĀRŪḌHA'),
 '“For the sage who wishes to climb to yoga, action is said to be the means; for one who has climbed to yoga, calm is said to be the means.”',
 [('ārurukṣoḥ muneḥ yogam','<b>for the sage wishing to climb to yoga</b>'),('karma kāraṇam ucyate','<b>action is called the means</b>'),('yoga-ārūḍhasya','<b>for one who has climbed to yoga</b>'),('śamaḥ kāraṇam ucyate','<b>calm is called the means</b>')],
 'Two stages: climbing (by action) and arriving (by calm).',
 'A clear map of the spiritual life. Beginners need work; the advanced need stillness. Śaṅkara reads it as a progression from karma yoga to renunciation; others as a shift of emphasis within one life.',
 'Know your stage. Effort first; ease later.',
 'Climb by work; <b>rest in calm</b>.'),
X(4,'WHEN HAS ONE ARRIVED?',
 S('A sage free of all cravings, sitting on a summit','Y','light','ĀRŪḌHA'),
 '“When one is not attached to sense objects or to actions, and has renounced all selfish schemes, then he is said to have climbed to yoga.”',
 [('yadā hi na indriya-artheṣu','<b>when not to sense objects</b>'),('na karmasu anuṣajjate','<b>nor to actions is attached</b>'),('sarva-saṅkalpa-sannyāsī','<b>renouncer of all schemes</b>'),('yoga-ārūḍhaḥ tadā ucyate','<b>then is called risen to yoga</b>')],
 'The mark of arrival: no attachment to objects or to action itself.',
 'Commentators note two attachments: to things, and to being busy. Both must go.',
 'Some of us are attached to being busy. Notice that too.',
 'Unattached to things <b>and to doing</b>.'),
X(5,'LIFT YOURSELF BY YOURSELF',
 S('A person pulling himself up a ladder by his own hand','P','mountain','UDDHARET'),
 '“Lift yourself by yourself; do not let yourself sink. For the self alone is the friend of the self, and the self alone is the enemy of the self.”',
 [('uddharet ātmanā ātmānam','<b>lift the self by the self</b>'),('na ātmānam avasādayet','<b>do not let the self sink</b>'),('ātmā eva hi ātmanaḥ bandhuḥ','<b>the self is the self’s friend</b>'),('ātmā eva ripuḥ ātmanaḥ','<b>the self is the self’s enemy</b>')],
 'One of the most practical verses of the Gītā: you are your own best friend and worst enemy.',
 'Here “self” (<i>ātman</i>) means one’s own mind and will. Commentators say no one else can do our inner work for us. Teachers guide; we must climb.',
 'Be your own friend today. Encourage yourself as you would a friend.',
 'You are <b>your own friend and enemy</b>.'),
X(6,'FRIEND OR ENEMY',
 S('Two figures: a mastered mind as friend, an unruly mind as enemy','P','mind','FRIEND · FOE'),
 '“The self is a friend to one who has conquered himself by himself; but for one who has not, the self acts like an enemy.”',
 [('bandhuḥ ātmā ātmanaḥ tasya','<b>the self is a friend to him</b>'),('yena ātmā eva ātmanā jitaḥ','<b>by whom the self is conquered by the self</b>'),('anātmanaḥ tu śatrutve','<b>but for the unconquered, in enmity</b>'),('varteta ātmā eva śatru-vat','<b>the self acts like an enemy</b>')],
 'A mastered mind helps; an unmastered one harms.',
 'Commentators: the same mind that drags us down, once trained, lifts us. Discipline turns an enemy into a friend.',
 'Train the mind gently, and it will serve you.',
 'A trained mind <b>is a friend</b>.'),
X(7,'CALM IN HEAT AND COLD',
 S('A calm person while heat, cold, praise and blame swirl','Y','scale','PRAŚĀNTA'),
 '“For the self-conquered and peaceful, the Supreme Self is steady in cold and heat, pleasure and pain, honour and dishonour.”',
 [('jita-ātmanaḥ praśāntasya','<b>of the self-conquered, peaceful</b>'),('parama-ātmā samāhitaḥ','<b>the Supreme Self is steadily present</b>'),('śīta-uṣṇa-sukha-duḥkheṣu','<b>in cold, heat, pleasure, pain</b>'),('tathā māna-apamānayoḥ','<b>and in honour and dishonour</b>')],
 'Peace holds through every pair of opposites, including praise and insult.',
 'Honour and dishonour are added to heat and cold: social opposites are often harder to bear. Commentators read <i>paramātmā samāhitaḥ</i> as “absorbed in the Supreme”.',
 'Praise and blame are weather too. Let them pass.',
 'Steady in <b>praise and blame</b>.'),
X(8,'GOLD AND CLAY ALIKE',
 S('A lump of clay, a stone and a gold coin side by side, a calm sage looking equally','Y','scale','SAMA'),
 '“The yogi content with knowledge and wisdom, steady, master of the senses, to whom a lump of earth, a stone and gold are the same, is said to be united.”',
 [('jñāna-vijñāna-tṛpta-ātmā','<b>content with knowledge and wisdom</b>'),('kūṭa-sthaḥ vijita-indriyaḥ','<b>steady, senses conquered</b>'),('sama-loṣṭa-aśma-kāñcanaḥ','<b>same to clod, stone, gold</b>'),('yuktaḥ iti ucyate yogī','<b>is called a united yogi</b>')],
 'To the yogi, earth, stone and gold have the same worth.',
 '<i>Kūṭastha</i>: like an anvil, which stays the same while things are hammered on it. Commentators say this is not ignorance of value, but freedom from greed.',
 'Money matters for living; it need not rule our heart.',
 'Clay, stone and gold <b>alike</b>.'),
X(9,'FRIEND AND FOE ALIKE',
 S('A sage looking at friends, enemies and strangers with the same kindness','Y','family','SAMA-BUDDHI'),
 '“One who is equal-minded toward well-wishers, friends, enemies, the indifferent, the neutral, the hateful, relatives, the good and the wicked, excels.”',
 [('suhṛt-mitra-ari-udāsīna','<b>well-wisher, friend, enemy, indifferent</b>'),('madhyastha-dveṣya-bandhuṣu','<b>neutral, hateful, relatives</b>'),('sādhuṣu api ca pāpeṣu','<b>the good and the wicked</b>'),('sama-buddhiḥ viśiṣyate','<b>the equal-minded excels</b>')],
 'Even harder than gold and clay: friend and foe alike.',
 'Equal-mindedness toward people is the highest test. Commentators note it does not mean treating everyone the same way, but holding no hatred and no favouritism in the heart.',
 'Try wishing well to someone you dislike. It frees you more than them.',
 'Friend and foe <b>with an equal heart</b>.'),
X(10,'ALONE, IN A QUIET PLACE',
 S('A yogi sitting alone in a quiet forest','Y','tree','EKĀKĪ'),
 '“Let the yogi constantly practise concentration, sitting alone in a secluded place, mind and body controlled, free from hope and from possessions.”',
 [('yogī yuñjīta satatam','<b>let the yogi constantly practise</b>'),('ātmānam rahasi sthitaḥ','<b>staying in a secluded place</b>'),('ekākī yata-citta-ātmā','<b>alone, mind and body controlled</b>'),('nirāśīḥ aparigrahaḥ','<b>without hopes or possessions</b>')],
 'Instructions for meditation begin: solitude, control, simplicity.',
 'The Gītā now becomes a meditation manual. Commentators note “constantly”: regular practice, not occasional bursts.',
 'Find a quiet corner and a regular time. That is half the battle.',
 'A quiet place, <b>regular practice</b>.'),
X(11,'THE SEAT',
 S('A clean seat of grass, deerskin and cloth on firm ground','','seed','ĀSANA'),
 '“In a clean place, setting up a firm seat for himself, neither too high nor too low, covered with cloth, deerskin and kuśa grass,”',
 [('śucau deśe','<b>in a clean place</b>'),('pratiṣṭhāpya sthiram āsanam','<b>setting up a firm seat</b>'),('na ati-ucchritam na ati-nīcam','<b>not too high nor too low</b>'),('caila-ajina-kuśa-uttaram','<b>with cloth, deerskin, kuśa grass</b>')],
 'Practical details: a clean place and a firm, moderate seat.',
 'Kuśa grass at the bottom, deerskin, then cloth on top: the traditional seat. Commentators say the point is cleanliness, stability and comfort; modern practitioners use a mat or cushion.',
 'Prepare a simple, clean, steady place to sit. It invites the mind to settle.',
 'A clean place, <b>a steady seat</b>.'),
X(12,'ONE-POINTED MIND',
 S('A yogi seated with a single point of focus','Y','light','EKĀGRA'),
 '“…sitting there on the seat, making the mind one-pointed, controlling thought and senses, let him practise yoga for the purification of the self.”',
 [('tatra ekāgram manaḥ kṛtvā','<b>there, making the mind one-pointed</b>'),('yata-citta-indriya-kriyaḥ','<b>controlling thought and senses</b>'),('upaviśya āsane','<b>sitting on the seat</b>'),('yuñjyāt yogam ātma-viśuddhaye','<b>practise yoga to purify the self</b>')],
 'The purpose of meditation: one-pointedness and purification.',
 '<i>Ekāgra</i>, one-pointed, is the key quality of a meditative mind in Patañjali’s Yoga Sūtra too. Commentators stress that the aim is purity, not powers.',
 'Choose one point: the breath, a word, a light. Return to it when the mind wanders.',
 'Make the mind <b>one-pointed</b>.'),
X(13,'BODY, HEAD AND NECK STRAIGHT',
 S('A yogi sitting upright with gaze at the tip of the nose','Y','mind','UPRIGHT'),
 '“Holding body, head and neck erect and still, steady, gazing at the tip of his nose, not looking around,”',
 [('samam kāya-śiraḥ-grīvam','<b>body, head and neck in line</b>'),('dhārayan acalam sthiraḥ','<b>holding unmoving, steady</b>'),('samprekṣya nāsika-agram svam','<b>gazing at the tip of his nose</b>'),('diśaḥ ca anavalokayan','<b>not looking around</b>')],
 'Posture: upright and still, gaze lowered.',
 'Commentators debate whether the gaze is literally at the nose tip or simply lowered and inward. Śaṅkara says the eyes are half-closed and turned within, not strained.',
 'Sit tall but relaxed. A steady body helps a steady mind.',
 'Sit upright, <b>still and steady</b>.'),
X(14,'MIND ON ME',
 S('A meditating yogi with a calm, fearless glow, Krishna in his heart','Y','heart','MAT-CITTAḤ'),
 '“Serene and fearless, firm in the vow of brahmacarya, controlling the mind, let him sit, his thought on me, intent on me, united.”',
 [('praśānta-ātmā vigata-bhīḥ','<b>serene, fearless</b>'),('brahmacāri-vrate sthitaḥ','<b>firm in the vow of brahmacarya</b>'),('manaḥ saṃyamya mat-cittaḥ','<b>mind controlled, thought on me</b>'),('yuktaḥ āsīta mat-paraḥ','<b>let him sit united, intent on me</b>')],
 'The inner attitude: calm, fearless, disciplined, centred on the Divine.',
 '<i>Brahmacarya</i> traditionally means celibacy and self-restraint; many modern teachers read it as moderation of the senses. Kṛṣṇa again says “on me”: meditation is devotional too.',
 'Bring trust and calm to your practice. Fear makes the mind jumpy.',
 'Serene, fearless, <b>mind on the Lord</b>.'),
X(15,'PEACE THAT ENDS IN FREEDOM',
 S('A yogi glowing with peace, the light rising beyond','Y','om','ŚĀNTIM'),
 '“Thus always uniting himself, the yogi of controlled mind reaches peace, the supreme nirvāṇa, which abides in me.”',
 [('yuñjan evam sadā ātmānam','<b>thus always uniting the self</b>'),('yogī niyata-mānasaḥ','<b>the yogi of controlled mind</b>'),('śāntim nirvāṇa-paramām','<b>peace, culminating in nirvāṇa</b>'),('mat-saṃsthām adhigacchati','<b>abiding in me, he reaches</b>')],
 'The goal of meditation: peace that rests in God.',
 '<i>Nirvāṇa</i> appears again (see 2.72, 5.24–26), here as resting in the Lord. Commentators note the Gītā’s nirvāṇa is fullness, not mere extinction.',
 'Regular practice leads somewhere: to a peace that deepens over time.',
 'Practice leads to <b>peace in God</b>.'),
X(16,'NOT TOO MUCH, NOT TOO LITTLE',
 S('A balance between a big plate and an empty plate, a bed and an alarm clock','','scale','BALANCE'),
 '“Yoga is not for one who eats too much, nor for one who eats nothing at all; nor for one who sleeps too much, nor for one who stays awake too long, Arjuna.”',
 [('na ati-aśnataḥ tu yogaḥ asti','<b>no yoga for one who overeats</b>'),('na ca ekāntam anaśnataḥ','<b>nor for one who eats nothing</b>'),('na ca ati-svapna-śīlasya','<b>nor for one who oversleeps</b>'),('jāgrataḥ na eva','<b>nor for one who stays awake too much</b>')],
 'The middle way: no extremes.',
 'A strikingly modern teaching. The Gītā rejects harsh austerity and indulgence alike. Commentators note this is close to the Buddha’s “middle path”.',
 'Eat moderately, sleep enough. A balanced body makes meditation possible.',
 'No yoga <b>in extremes</b>.'),
X(17,'BALANCED LIVING',
 S('A daily wheel of eating, working, sleeping and waking in balance','','wheel','YUKTA'),
 '“For one who is moderate in eating and recreation, moderate in effort at work, moderate in sleep and waking, yoga becomes the destroyer of sorrow.”',
 [('yukta-āhāra-vihārasya','<b>moderate in food and recreation</b>'),('yukta-ceṣṭasya karmasu','<b>moderate in effort at work</b>'),('yukta-svapna-avabodhasya','<b>moderate in sleep and waking</b>'),('yogaḥ bhavati duḥkha-hā','<b>yoga destroys sorrow</b>')],
 'Balance in food, play, work and sleep makes yoga effective.',
 'This verse is a favourite for its practical wisdom. The word <i>yukta</i>, balanced or fitting, is the same root as yoga. Commentators say balance is itself a kind of yoga.',
 'Check your balance: food, rest, work, play. Small adjustments help the mind too.',
 'Balanced living <b>destroys sorrow</b>.'),
X(18,'SETTLED IN THE SELF',
 S('A mind settling like silt in still water','Y','light','NIḤSPṚHA'),
 '“When the disciplined mind rests in the Self alone, free from longing for all desires, then one is called united (yukta).”',
 [('yadā viniyatam cittam','<b>when the controlled mind</b>'),('ātmani eva avatiṣṭhate','<b>rests in the Self alone</b>'),('niḥspṛhaḥ sarva-kāmebhyaḥ','<b>free from longing for all desires</b>'),('yuktaḥ iti ucyate tadā','<b>then he is called united</b>')],
 'The meditative state: mind resting in the Self, without craving.',
 'Commentators describe the shift from controlling the mind to the mind resting naturally. Effort gives way to ease.',
 'Moments of rest in yourself, without wanting anything, are glimpses of this state.',
 'Mind resting <b>in the Self</b>.'),
X(19,'A LAMP IN A WINDLESS PLACE',
 S('A lamp flame perfectly still, no wind','','lamp','NIVĀTA-STHA',NIGHT2),
 '“‘As a lamp in a windless place does not flicker’: that is the image used for the yogi of controlled mind, practising union with the Self.”',
 [('yathā dīpaḥ nivāta-sthaḥ','<b>as a lamp in a windless place</b>'),('na iṅgate','<b>does not flicker</b>'),('sā upamā smṛtā','<b>that is the image remembered</b>'),('yoginaḥ yata-cittasya','<b>of the yogi of controlled mind</b>')],
 'The most famous image of meditation in the Gītā.',
 'The steady flame is both still and bright: calm without dullness. Commentators note “windless”: shielded from the winds of desire. The image appears across Indian meditation traditions.',
 'Picture a candle flame, perfectly still. Let the mind become like that, for a breath or two.',
 'Like a <b>lamp in a windless place</b>.'),
X(20,'WHERE THE MIND IS STILLED',
 S('A meditator seeing the Self within, a deep calm','Y','light','UPARAMATE'),
 '“When the mind, restrained by the practice of yoga, comes to rest, and when, seeing the Self by the Self, one is content in the Self,”',
 [('yatra uparamate cittam','<b>where the mind comes to rest</b>'),('niruddham yoga-sevayā','<b>restrained by practice of yoga</b>'),('yatra ca eva ātmanā ātmānam','<b>and where, by the Self, the Self</b>'),('paśyan ātmani tuṣyati','<b>seeing, one is content in the Self</b>')],
 'The beginning of a description of samādhi, continued in the next verses.',
 'This verse uses the word <i>niruddha</i>, restrained, the same root as Patañjali’s definition of yoga (citta-vṛtti-nirodha). Commentators see the Gītā and the Yoga Sūtra pointing to the same experience.',
 'Stillness is not empty. In it something is seen and enjoyed.',
 'In stillness, <b>the Self sees itself</b>.'),
X(21,'JOY BEYOND THE SENSES',
 S('A meditator with a deep inner joy beyond the senses','Y','heart','ATĪNDRIYA'),
 '“…where one knows that infinite joy which is grasped by the intellect and beyond the senses, and established there, never moves from the truth,”',
 [('sukham ātyantikam yat tat','<b>that infinite joy</b>'),('buddhi-grāhyam atīndriyam','<b>grasped by intellect, beyond senses</b>'),('vetti yatra','<b>where one knows</b>'),('na ca eva ayam sthitaḥ calati tattvataḥ','<b>established, never moves from truth</b>')],
 'A joy that the senses cannot give.',
 'The joy of meditation is <i>ātyantika</i>, endless, not tied to objects. Commentators say once known, it anchors one firmly in truth.',
 'Seek joys that don’t depend on the senses. They last.',
 'An <b>infinite joy beyond the senses</b>.'),
X(22,'NO GREATER GAIN',
 S('A sage unmoved even by great sorrow, a treasure in his heart','Y','gift','NO GREATER GAIN'),
 '“…having gained which, one thinks there is no greater gain, and established in which, one is not shaken even by great sorrow,”',
 [('yam labdhvā ca aparam lābham','<b>having gained which, another gain</b>'),('manyate na adhikam tataḥ','<b>one thinks none greater</b>'),('yasmin sthitaḥ','<b>established in which</b>'),('na duḥkhena guruṇā api vicālyate','<b>is not shaken even by great sorrow</b>')],
 'The supreme gain that makes all others small.',
 'Not even great sorrow shakes one established here. Commentators: this is the security every person seeks in money, power or people, found in its true form.',
 'What would make you unshakeable? The Gītā says: this inner treasure.',
 'No gain <b>is greater</b>.'),
X(23,'FREEDOM FROM UNION WITH SORROW',
 S('A chain labelled sorrow breaking','Y','chain','DUḤKHA-SAṂYOGA-VIYOGA'),
 '“Let that be known as yoga: the breaking of union with sorrow. This yoga should be practised with determination and an undespairing mind.”',
 [('tam vidyāt duḥkha-saṃyoga-viyogam','<b>know that as separation from union with sorrow</b>'),('yoga-saṃjñitam','<b>called yoga</b>'),('saḥ niścayena yoktavyaḥ yogaḥ','<b>this yoga should be practised with resolve</b>'),('anirviṇṇa-cetasā','<b>with an undespairing mind</b>')],
 'A beautiful definition: yoga is dis-union from sorrow.',
 'A play on words: yoga means union, yet here it is the <i>viyoga</i>, separation, from sorrow. Commentators praise the call to practise “without despair”: progress is slow, and patience is part of it.',
 'Keep practising, even when progress seems slow. Don’t give up.',
 'Yoga is <b>freedom from sorrow</b>.'),
X(24,'GIVE UP DESIRES BORN OF SCHEMING',
 S('A person setting down a basket of wants, closing the gates of the senses','Y','mind','LET GO'),
 '“Giving up completely all desires born of selfish scheming, and restraining the whole group of senses on every side by the mind alone,”',
 [('saṅkalpa-prabhavān kāmān','<b>desires born of scheming</b>'),('tyaktvā sarvān aśeṣataḥ','<b>giving up all completely</b>'),('manasā eva indriya-grāmam','<b>by the mind alone, the group of senses</b>'),('viniyamya samantataḥ','<b>restraining on all sides</b>')],
 'Practical instruction: drop desires, rein in the senses.',
 'Desires born of <i>saṅkalpa</i>, imagining and planning. Commentators: stop feeding imagination with wants, and desires starve.',
 'Notice when you are daydreaming about wants. Gently stop feeding the dream.',
 'Stop feeding <b>desires born of fantasy</b>.'),
X(25,'LITTLE BY LITTLE',
 S('A person climbing step by step, slowly','Y','mountain','ŚANAIḤ ŚANAIḤ'),
 '“Little by little, let him become still, with understanding held firm; making the mind rest in the Self, let him think of nothing else.”',
 [('śanaiḥ śanaiḥ uparamet','<b>little by little, let him become still</b>'),('buddhyā dhṛti-gṛhītayā','<b>with understanding held firm</b>'),('ātma-saṃstham manaḥ kṛtvā','<b>making the mind rest in the Self</b>'),('na kiñcit api cintayet','<b>let him think of nothing else</b>')],
 '<i>Śanaiḥ śanaiḥ</i>: slowly, slowly. Patience is the method.',
 'A gentle, realistic instruction. Commentators say forcing stillness backfires; gradual practice, with steady will (<i>dhṛti</i>), works.',
 'Be patient with yourself. Stillness comes gradually.',
 '<b>Little by little</b>, become still.'),
X(26,'BRING IT BACK',
 S('A mind wandering off and being gently led back','Y','mind','BRING IT BACK'),
 '“Wherever the restless, unsteady mind wanders, from there let him restrain it and bring it back under the control of the Self.”',
 [('yataḥ yataḥ niścarati','<b>wherever it wanders</b>'),('manaḥ cañcalam asthiram','<b>the restless, unsteady mind</b>'),('tataḥ tataḥ niyamya etat','<b>from there restraining it</b>'),('ātmani eva vaśam nayet','<b>bring it under control of the Self</b>')],
 'The core instruction of all meditation: when the mind wanders, bring it back.',
 'Every meditator knows this. The mind will wander; the practice is to notice and return, again and again, without anger.',
 'Each time you bring the mind back, that is a successful moment of practice.',
 'When it wanders, <b>bring it back</b>.'),
X(27,'SUPREME JOY',
 S('A yogi radiating joy, the dust of passion settled','Y','light','UTTAMAM SUKHAM'),
 '“Supreme joy comes to the yogi whose mind is at peace, whose passion is quieted, who is free from sin and has become Brahman.”',
 [('praśānta-manasam hi enam','<b>to him whose mind is at peace</b>'),('yoginam sukham uttamam','<b>supreme joy to the yogi</b>'),('upaiti śānta-rajasam','<b>comes, with passion quieted</b>'),('brahma-bhūtam akalmaṣam','<b>become Brahman, sinless</b>')],
 'The reward of meditation: supreme joy.',
 '<i>Rajas</i>, restless passion, settles like dust when the wind stops. Commentators describe joy arising naturally once agitation ends.',
 'Calm itself is joyful. Quiet the restlessness and joy appears.',
 'Peace brings <b>supreme joy</b>.'),
X(28,'TOUCHING BRAHMAN',
 S('A yogi touching a great light with ease','Y','om','BRAHMA-SAṂSPARŚA'),
 '“Thus always uniting himself, the yogi freed from sin easily enjoys the infinite joy of contact with Brahman.”',
 [('yuñjan evam sadā ātmānam','<b>thus always uniting the self</b>'),('yogī vigata-kalmaṣaḥ','<b>the yogi freed from sin</b>'),('sukhena brahma-saṃsparśam','<b>easily, the contact with Brahman</b>'),('atyantam sukham aśnute','<b>enjoys infinite joy</b>')],
 'Contact with Brahman brings infinite joy, and it becomes easy.',
 '<i>Sukhena</i>, easily: what was effortful becomes natural. Commentators call this the fruit of long practice.',
 'What is hard at first becomes easy with practice. Stay with it.',
 'The joy of <b>touching Brahman</b>.'),
X(29,'SELF IN ALL, ALL IN SELF',
 S('A yogi seeing all beings in himself and himself in all','Y','family','SARVATRA'),
 '“With the self united in yoga, seeing the same everywhere, he sees the Self in all beings and all beings in the Self.”',
 [('sarva-bhūta-stham ātmānam','<b>the Self dwelling in all beings</b>'),('sarva-bhūtāni ca ātmani','<b>and all beings in the Self</b>'),('īkṣate yoga-yukta-ātmā','<b>sees, the one united in yoga</b>'),('sarvatra sama-darśanaḥ','<b>seeing the same everywhere</b>')],
 'The vision of unity: the Self in all, all in the Self.',
 'This echoes the Īśa Upaniṣad (6): one who sees all beings in the Self and the Self in all beings hates no one. Commentators of every school treasure this verse.',
 'Look at someone and try to sense the same life in them as in you.',
 'See <b>the Self in all beings</b>.'),
X(30,'NEVER LOST TO ME',
 S('Krishna and a devotee always seeing each other','KA','heart','NEVER LOST'),
 '“One who sees me everywhere and sees everything in me: I am never lost to him, nor is he ever lost to me.”',
 [('yaḥ mām paśyati sarvatra','<b>who sees me everywhere</b>'),('sarvam ca mayi paśyati','<b>and sees all in me</b>'),('tasya aham na praṇaśyāmi','<b>I am not lost to him</b>'),('saḥ ca me na praṇaśyati','<b>nor is he lost to me</b>')],
 'A tender promise: God and the devotee never lose each other.',
 'Rāmānuja’s school treasures this verse as the heart of devotional seeing. Śaṅkara reads “me” as the Self in all. Either way, separation ends.',
 'Seeing the Divine everywhere means never being alone.',
 'Never lost <b>to each other</b>.'),
X(31,'WORSHIPPING ME IN ALL',
 S('A devotee serving people, seeing Krishna in each','P','family','IN ALL BEINGS'),
 '“The yogi who, established in oneness, worships me as dwelling in all beings, whatever his way of life, lives in me.”',
 [('sarva-bhūta-sthitam yaḥ mām','<b>who me, dwelling in all beings</b>'),('bhajati ekatvam āsthitaḥ','<b>worships, established in oneness</b>'),('sarvathā vartamānaḥ api','<b>in whatever way he lives</b>'),('saḥ yogī mayi vartate','<b>that yogi lives in me</b>')],
 'Serving God in all beings, whatever one’s outer life.',
 '“Whatever his way of life”: monk or householder. Commentators note this as a charter for engaged spirituality; Vivekananda often spoke of serving God in human beings.',
 'Serving people is serving the Divine in them.',
 'Worship the Lord <b>in all beings</b>.'),
X(32,'THEIR JOY, THEIR PAIN',
 S('A person feeling another’s pain as his own','Y','heart','ĀTMAUPAMYA'),
 '“One who, by comparison with himself, sees the same everywhere, Arjuna, whether in pleasure or pain, is considered the highest yogi.”',
 [('ātma-aupamyena sarvatra','<b>by comparison with oneself, everywhere</b>'),('samam paśyati yaḥ arjuna','<b>who sees equally</b>'),('sukham vā yadi vā duḥkham','<b>whether pleasure or pain</b>'),('saḥ yogī paramaḥ mataḥ','<b>he is the highest yogi</b>')],
 'The highest yogi feels others’ joy and pain as his own.',
 '<i>Ātmaupamya</i>, “by comparison with oneself”: the golden rule in Gītā form. Commentators say the highest yogi is defined by empathy, not by powers.',
 'Before acting, ask: how would this feel if done to me?',
 'Feel others’ joy and pain <b>as your own</b>.'),
X(33,'“THE MIND IS RESTLESS”',
 S('Arjuna watching a monkey-like mind jumping around','A','mind','CAÑCALA!'),
 'Arjuna said: This yoga of evenness that you have taught, Madhusūdana, I do not see how it can last, because the mind is restless.',
 [('yaḥ ayam yogaḥ tvayā proktaḥ','<b>this yoga taught by you</b>'),('sāmyena madhusūdana','<b>of evenness</b>'),('etasya aham na paśyāmi','<b>of this I do not see</b>'),('cañcalatvāt sthitim sthirām','<b>steady duration, because of restlessness</b>')],
 'Arjuna voices every meditator’s complaint: the mind won’t stay still.',
 'Commentators love Arjuna’s honesty here. He speaks for all of us. Kṛṣṇa’s answer will not deny the problem.',
 'If your mind is restless, you are in good company: even Arjuna said so.',
 'Arjuna: <b>the mind is so restless!</b>'),
X(34,'HARDER THAN HOLDING THE WIND',
 S('Arjuna trying to catch the wind in his hands','A','storm','LIKE THE WIND'),
 '“For the mind is restless, Kṛṣṇa, turbulent, strong and obstinate. I think it is as hard to control as the wind.”',
 [('cañcalam hi manaḥ kṛṣṇa','<b>the mind is restless, Kṛṣṇa</b>'),('pramāthi balavat dṛḍham','<b>turbulent, strong, obstinate</b>'),('tasya aham nigraham manye','<b>its control I think</b>'),('vāyoḥ iva su-duṣkaram','<b>as very hard as the wind’s</b>')],
 'Arjuna compares the mind to the wind: impossible to hold.',
 'Four words for the mind: restless, turbulent, strong, stubborn. Commentators note that Kṛṣṇa’s next verse accepts every one of them.',
 'Name your mind’s habits honestly. That’s the first step to working with them.',
 'The mind is <b>like the wind</b>.'),
X(35,'PRACTICE AND DETACHMENT',
 S('Krishna smiling, showing two tools: practice and detachment','K','book','ABHYĀSA · VAIRĀGYA'),
 'The Lord said: Without doubt, mighty-armed one, the mind is restless and hard to control. But by practice and by detachment, son of Kuntī, it can be held.',
 [('asaṃśayam mahā-bāho','<b>no doubt, mighty-armed one</b>'),('manaḥ durnigraham calam','<b>the mind is hard to control, restless</b>'),('abhyāsena tu kaunteya','<b>but by practice</b>'),('vairāgyeṇa ca gṛhyate','<b>and by detachment, it is held</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa agrees, then gives the two keys: practice (<i>abhyāsa</i>) and detachment (<i>vairāgya</i>).',
 'The same two keys appear in Patañjali’s Yoga Sūtra (1.12). Commentators: practice means steady, repeated effort; detachment means loosening the pull of desires. Together they tame the wind.',
 'Practise regularly; want less. Those two habits change the mind.',
 '<b>Practice and detachment</b> tame the mind.'),
X(36,'HARD, BUT POSSIBLE',
 S('A disciplined person reaching a summit by right means','Y','mountain','POSSIBLE'),
 '“Yoga is hard to attain for one who is not self-controlled, I agree; but by one who is self-controlled and strives by the right means, it can be attained.”',
 [('asaṃyata-ātmanā yogaḥ','<b>for the uncontrolled, yoga</b>'),('duṣprāpaḥ iti me matiḥ','<b>is hard to attain, I think</b>'),('vaśya-ātmanā tu yatatā','<b>but by the self-controlled who strives</b>'),('śakyaḥ avāptum upāyataḥ','<b>it can be attained by right means</b>')],
 'Encouragement: hard, but possible with discipline and right method.',
 '<i>Upāya</i>, the right means, matters: effort alone is not enough; it must be skilful. Commentators note Kṛṣṇa does not promise ease, only possibility.',
 'Right method plus steady effort works. Find a good method and stay with it.',
 'Hard, <b>but possible</b>.'),
X(37,'WHAT IF ONE FALLS SHORT?',
 S('Arjuna worried about a seeker who started but fell short','A','mountain','AND IF I FAIL?'),
 'Arjuna said: One who has faith but does not strive enough, whose mind falls away from yoga, not reaching perfection: what path does he go, Kṛṣṇa?',
 [('ayatiḥ śraddhayā upetaḥ','<b>not striving, though having faith</b>'),('yogāt calita-mānasaḥ','<b>mind fallen from yoga</b>'),('aprāpya yoga-saṃsiddhim','<b>not attaining perfection in yoga</b>'),('kām gatim kṛṣṇa gacchati','<b>what goal does he reach?</b>')],
 'Arjuna asks the question every sincere beginner fears: what if I fail?',
 'A very human worry: what if I try and fall short? Commentators note Arjuna asks for all of us.',
 'Fear of failure stops many from starting. Hear Kṛṣṇa’s answer.',
 'Arjuna: <b>what if I fall short?</b>'),
X(38,'LIKE A SCATTERED CLOUD?',
 S('A small cloud breaking apart between two skies','A','storm','LOST?'),
 '“Fallen from both, does he not perish like a scattered cloud, mighty-armed one, without support, confused on the path of Brahman?”',
 [('kaccit na ubhaya-vibhraṣṭaḥ','<b>fallen from both, does he not</b>'),('chinna-abhram iva naśyati','<b>perish like a scattered cloud?</b>'),('apratiṣṭhaḥ mahā-bāho','<b>without support</b>'),('vimūḍhaḥ brahmaṇaḥ pathi','<b>confused on the path of Brahman</b>')],
 'Arjuna fears such a person loses both worldly life and spiritual goal.',
 'A vivid image: a cloud torn from one mass that fails to join another, and dissolves. Commentators: the fear of losing both worlds is common to beginners.',
 'Worry about “wasting” effort is natural. The Gītā will reassure.',
 'Lost <b>like a scattered cloud?</b>'),
X(39,'“ONLY YOU CAN ANSWER”',
 S('Arjuna appealing to Krishna as the only one who can remove his doubt','A','light','ONLY YOU'),
 '“Please remove this doubt of mine completely, Kṛṣṇa. For no one but you can remove it.”',
 [('etat me saṃśayam kṛṣṇa','<b>this doubt of mine, Kṛṣṇa</b>'),('chettum arhasi aśeṣataḥ','<b>you should cut completely</b>'),('tvat-anyaḥ saṃśayasya asya','<b>other than you, of this doubt</b>'),('chettā na hi upapadyate','<b>no remover can be found</b>')],
 'Arjuna asks Kṛṣṇa to cut this doubt.',
 'The verb is the same as in 4.42: cut the doubt. Commentators note the trust: Arjuna turns to the one he believes can answer.',
 'Take your deepest doubts to the wisest source you know.',
 'Arjuna asks <b>the one who knows</b>.'),
X(40,'NO DOER OF GOOD COMES TO GRIEF',
 S('Krishna reassuring Arjuna, a safe path glowing beneath a seeker','K','heart','NEVER LOST'),
 'The Lord said: Pārtha, there is no destruction for him, neither here nor hereafter. For no one who does good, my friend, ever comes to a bad end.',
 [('pārtha na eva iha na amutra','<b>Pārtha, neither here nor hereafter</b>'),('vināśaḥ tasya vidyate','<b>is there ruin for him</b>'),('na hi kalyāṇa-kṛt kaścit','<b>no one who does good</b>'),('durgatim tāta gacchati','<b>goes to a bad end, my friend</b>')],
 'One of the most comforting verses in the Gītā.',
 'Kṛṣṇa calls Arjuna <i>tāta</i>, an affectionate word, used for a son or dear one. The promise: no sincere effort toward good is ever lost (compare 2.40).',
 'Every step you take toward good stays with you. Nothing sincere is wasted.',
 'No doer of good <b>comes to grief</b>.',
 ("Even if I give up halfway?","Even then. Kṛṣṇa says the good you did carries you forward, in this life or the next.")),
X(41,'BORN IN A GOOD HOME',
 S('A child born into a kind, prosperous family, a glow above','','family','A NEW START'),
 '“Having reached the worlds of the righteous and lived there many years, the one who fell from yoga is born in the home of the pure and prosperous.”',
 [('prāpya puṇya-kṛtām lokān','<b>reaching the worlds of the righteous</b>'),('uṣitvā śāśvatīḥ samāḥ','<b>living there countless years</b>'),('śucīnām śrīmatām gehe','<b>in the home of the pure and prosperous</b>'),('yoga-bhraṣṭaḥ abhijāyate','<b>the one fallen from yoga is born</b>')],
 'The fallen seeker is rewarded and reborn into favourable circumstances.',
 'The Gītā’s picture of rebirth here is gentle: good effort leads to a good start next time. Commentators note “pure and prosperous”: a home where both spiritual and material support are present.',
 'Good circumstances can be the fruit of past effort. Use them well.',
 'A good start, <b>in a good home</b>.'),
X(42,'OR IN A FAMILY OF YOGIS',
 S('A child born among wise yogis','','sage','RARE'),
 '“Or he is born in a family of wise yogis. Such a birth is very hard to gain in this world.”',
 [('atha vā yoginām eva','<b>or else, of yogis</b>'),('kule bhavati dhīmatām','<b>in a family of the wise</b>'),('etat hi durlabhataram','<b>this indeed is rarer</b>'),('loke janma yat īdṛśam','<b>such a birth in the world</b>')],
 'An even better rebirth: among yogis.',
 'Commentators say this is rarer and better: growing up surrounded by wisdom. Śaṅkara notes these are poor in wealth but rich in knowledge.',
 'A home of wisdom is a rare gift. Create one for others.',
 'Born among <b>the wise</b>.'),
X(43,'PICKING UP WHERE YOU LEFT OFF',
 S('A child picking up a book left open from a past life','P','book','CONTINUE'),
 '“There he regains the understanding of his former life, and strives again for perfection, joy of the Kurus.”',
 [('tatra tam buddhi-saṃyogam','<b>there, that union with understanding</b>'),('labhate paurva-dehikam','<b>he regains, from the former body</b>'),('yatate ca tataḥ bhūyaḥ','<b>and strives again from there</b>'),('saṃsiddhau kuru-nandana','<b>for perfection</b>')],
 'Progress carries over from life to life.',
 'Commentators explain natural gifts and early spiritual interest this way: they are the fruit of past effort. Nothing is lost.',
 'Your interests and gifts may be seeds from long ago. Water them.',
 'Pick up <b>where you left off</b>.'),
X(44,'DRAWN ON ALMOST AGAINST HIS WILL',
 S('A person pulled along a path by an invisible current','P','river','PULLED ON'),
 '“By that former practice he is carried on, even against his will. Even one who merely wishes to know yoga goes beyond the Vedic ritual.”',
 [('pūrva-abhyāsena tena eva','<b>by that very former practice</b>'),('hriyate hi avaśaḥ api saḥ','<b>he is carried, even helplessly</b>'),('jijñāsuḥ api yogasya','<b>even one who wishes to know yoga</b>'),('śabda-brahma ativartate','<b>goes beyond the Vedic word</b>')],
 'Past practice carries one forward, like a current.',
 '<i>Śabda-brahma</i>, the Veda as words, especially ritual. Even curiosity about yoga takes one beyond it. Commentators find great encouragement here.',
 'Even curiosity counts. Interest itself is a step on the path.',
 'Past practice <b>carries you forward</b>.'),
X(45,'OVER MANY LIVES',
 S('A long path through many lives ending in light','Y','light','ANEKA-JANMA'),
 '“The yogi who strives diligently, cleansed of sin, perfected through many births, then reaches the highest goal.”',
 [('prayatnāt yatamānaḥ tu','<b>but striving with effort</b>'),('yogī saṃśuddha-kilbiṣaḥ','<b>the yogi, cleansed of sin</b>'),('aneka-janma-saṃsiddhaḥ','<b>perfected over many births</b>'),('tataḥ yāti parām gatim','<b>then reaches the highest goal</b>')],
 'Perfection may take many lives, but it comes.',
 'Commentators note both effort and time. The view is long and patient; no life is wasted.',
 'Don’t measure progress by days. The journey is long, and every step counts.',
 'Over many lives, <b>the goal is reached</b>.'),
X(46,'THE YOGI IS GREATER',
 S('A yogi standing above an ascetic, a scholar and a ritualist','Y','light','BE A YOGI'),
 '“The yogi is greater than ascetics, greater even than the learned, and greater than those who do rituals. So be a yogi, Arjuna.”',
 [('tapasvibhyaḥ adhikaḥ yogī','<b>the yogi is greater than ascetics</b>'),('jñānibhyaḥ api mataḥ adhikaḥ','<b>greater even than the learned</b>'),('karmibhyaḥ ca adhikaḥ','<b>and greater than ritualists</b>'),('tasmāt yogī bhava arjuna','<b>so be a yogi, Arjuna</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa’s call: be a yogi.',
 'Commentators clarify that “the learned” here means those with book learning, not realisation. The yogi unites knowledge, action and devotion.',
 'Don’t settle for being only a doer, or only a thinker. Unite them.',
 '<b>Be a yogi</b>, Arjuna.'),
X(47,'THE BEST OF YOGIS',
 S('A devotee with Krishna in his heart, worshipping with faith','KA','heart','MAD-GATENA'),
 '“And of all yogis, the one who worships me with faith, his inner self absorbed in me: him I consider the most united.”',
 [('yoginām api sarveṣām','<b>of all yogis</b>'),('mat-gatena antar-ātmanā','<b>with inner self gone to me</b>'),('śraddhāvān bhajate yaḥ mām','<b>who worships me with faith</b>'),('saḥ me yuktatamaḥ mataḥ','<b>he is the most united, I think</b>')],
 'The chapter ends with devotion as the crown of yoga.',
 'This verse leads into the next six chapters, which centre on devotion and knowledge of God. Rāmānuja’s school sees it as the key that makes meditation devotional.',
 'Love completes practice. Meditation with devotion is the highest yoga.',
 'The best yogi <b>is the devotee</b>.'),
]
cover=('<svg width="380" height="268" viewBox="0 0 380 268" role="img" aria-label="A yogi seated in meditation on a simple seat in a forest, a steady lamp flame beside him in a windless night">'
 '<rect width="380" height="268" fill="#0b2a4a" rx="8"/>'+STARS(14,3).replace('<text x="','<text x="1')+GLOW(190,140,90,'#ffd60a',.25)+TREE(60,250,1.6)+TREE(330,250,1.4)+'<path d="M0 240 h380 V268 H0z" fill="#2a7a4a" stroke="#111"/>'+'<rect x="130" y="226" width="120" height="16" fill="#c9a26b" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+YOGI(190,214,2)+LAMP(270,222,1.6)+'</svg>')
P=dict(ch=6,nv=47,V=V,per=[16,16,15],out='/home/claude/work/gita/Gita_Chapter06.html',
 title='Meditation · Bhagavad Gītā Chapter 6 · srini.presents',
 desc='Bhagavad Gītā chapter 6, Dhyāna Yoga, every śloka explained: lift yourself by yourself, how to sit and meditate, the middle path in food and sleep, the lamp in a windless place, taming the restless mind with practice and detachment, and the fate of one who falls short. All 47 ślokas in IAST, each on two pages.',
 burst='CHAPTER 6 OF 18',h1='MEDITATION',series='BHAGAVAD GĪTĀ · CHAPTER 6 · DHYĀNA YOGA',tag='A lamp in a windless place · every śloka explained',cover=cover,
 where=[f'In {chl(5)} Kṛṣṇa showed that true renunciation is inner, and ended with a first glimpse of meditation (5.27–28).',
  'Chapter 6 is the Gītā’s meditation manual: where to sit, how to sit, how much to eat and sleep, and what to do when the mind wanders. Arjuna protests that the mind is like the wind; Kṛṣṇa agrees, and gives two keys: practice and detachment. And he promises that no sincere effort is ever lost.'],
 wtalk=("My mind won't sit still.","Neither would Arjuna's. Chapter 6 is for exactly that problem."),
 fullnote=FULLNOTE,
 secs=[('THE TRUE YOGI',1),('YOUR OWN FRIEND',5),('HOW TO MEDITATE',10),('THE GOAL',18),('THE RESTLESS MIND',33),('IF ONE FALLS SHORT',37)],
 end=['<b>The true yogi (6.1–4).</b> Renunciation is inner; climb by work, rest in calm.',
  '<b>Your own friend (6.5–9).</b> Lift yourself by yourself; equal to gold and clay, friend and foe.',
  '<b>How to meditate (6.10–17).</b> A quiet place, a firm seat, an upright body, a mind on the Lord; moderation in food and sleep.',
  '<b>The goal (6.18–32).</b> A lamp in a windless place; joy beyond the senses; the Self in all beings.',
  '<b>The restless mind (6.33–47).</b> Tame it by practice and detachment; no doer of good comes to grief; the devotee is the best yogi.'],
 endsub='Chapter 6 in five lines',
 next=("What's next?",f'{chl(7)}: Knowing and realising. Kṛṣṇa begins to reveal himself: his two natures, everything strung on him like pearls on a thread, and the four kinds of people who seek him.'),
 endkey='<b>practise, let go, and the mind becomes a steady flame</b>.',
 foot=FOOT(6))
build2(P)
